WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.430
That's like saying you're a tourist who's
never been to the United States, and you're

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.690
coming for the first time.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:06.690
What can you learn?

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.820
Well, there's all kinds of things you can
learn.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.169
You can go to New York.

00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:12.049
You can go to a national park.

00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:13.539
You can go to a mountain.

00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:19.130
And then, if you get a chance to come back
briefly two years later, you'd want to come

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.130
back again.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.410
Well, it's the same thing for the solar corona.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.230
It's a part of the sun — and billions of
trillions of other of other stars — and

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.440
we can see it only at an eclipse.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.359
Each time we look at it, it's different because
the sun is different.

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:32.470
It varies with the sun-spot cycle.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.450
There are eruptions that go off, and things
change all the time.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:42.480
So since we only get a few minutes every couple
of years, we look at its motions.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.720
We look at its image.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.720
We look at its spectrum.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.960
We look a thousand different things as much
as we can in the brief period that we're allowed at an eclipse.

